annot(#noCore)

#noCore
# An arrow written from a class.
  `(_ -> S) -> T..` says every member of the open class [S] converts to a [T]: [T] is in [S] already, and
  [P] converts by its own arrow. So a function over [S] may be written for a [T] alone, and a [P]
  given to it converts first. That is what lets a class hold several representations of one thing
  and define what it does on one of them.
  [checkP] is the conversion at a call, [g] a definition passing on what it was given, and
  [checkPick] a value only known to be an [S] until it is computed. Written without the core
  library, so that when a compiler change breaks it there are a few lines to read rather than a
  standard library. `classArrowRun.ct` runs the same shapes.
class(S)

data(T(v -> Yes))
data(P)
data(Yes)
data(No)

class(YN, [Yes, No])

every(T.., isa= [/S])
every(P, isa= [/S])

(_ -> S) -> T..

P= T(v= Yes)

f(x -> S) -> Yes
f(x= T(v))= v

checkT= f(x= T(v= Yes))

checkP= f(x= P)

g(s -> S) -> Yes
g(s)= f(x= s)

checkG= g(s= P)

pick(b -> YN) -> S
pick(b= Yes)= P
pick(b= No)= T(v= Yes)

checkPick(b -> YN) -> Yes
checkPick(b)= f(x= pick(b= b))
